---
import Article from '../layouts/Article.astro';

const phases = [
  {
    name: 'Phase 1 — Due diligence',
    when: 'Before signatures',
    body: 'Budget, contract, schedule, and team reviewed before anything is binding. Allowances and exclusions surfaced, contract terms tested against what happens when things move, the consultant team assessed for gaps.',
    deliverables: 'Written audit report · risk register · negotiation agenda for your lawyer',
  },
  {
    name: 'Phase 2 — Pre-construction',
    when: 'Signatures to mobilization',
    body: 'The project set up to be verifiable: schedule of values structured so draws can be checked, reporting cadence agreed, change-order protocol fixed in writing, communication lines established so nothing important travels by hallway conversation.',
    deliverables: 'Baseline budget and schedule · draw verification protocol · reporting calendar',
  },
  {
    name: 'Phase 3 — Construction oversight',
    when: 'Ground break to substantial completion',
    body: 'The standing rhythm: draws verified against work in place before release, change orders reviewed and tracked against contingency, schedule monitored against the baseline, and a plain-language report to you on a fixed cadence — including the things going well.',
    deliverables: 'Draw verification reports · change-order ledger · monthly owner’s report',
  },
  {
    name: 'Phase 4 — Closeout',
    when: 'Substantial completion to final payment',
    body: 'The stage most owners are too exhausted to fight: deficiency lists driven to completion, holdbacks and lien periods managed to the calendar, warranties, manuals, and as-builts collected before the final cheque — the last leverage a project has.',
    deliverables: 'Deficiency log · closeout checklist · document archive',
  },
  {
    name: 'Phase 5 — Warranty',
    when: 'The first year and beyond',
    body: 'Warranty claims documented, submitted, and pursued while the obligations are still live. The file we built during construction becomes the evidence that makes warranty conversations short.',
    deliverables: 'Warranty claim file · anniversary inspection coordination',
  },
];
---

<Article
  title="How We Work — Five Phases"
  heading="How we work"
  kicker="Method"
  description="Owner’s representation in five phases, from due diligence before signatures through warranty — with the deliverables named for each."
>
  <p>
    Representation is a discipline, not a presence. Each phase below has named deliverables — documents you
    receive, not assurances you are given. Engagements can begin at any phase, but the earlier phases are
    where the leverage lives: everything after signatures is played from the position the paperwork created.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose mt-10">
    {
      phases.map((phase) => (
        <section class="border-t border-rule py-8" aria-label={phase.name}>
          <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-[16rem_1fr]">
            <div>
              <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">{phase.name}</h2>
              <p class="annotation mt-1">{phase.when}</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="max-w-prose text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">{phase.body}</p>
              <p class="annotation mt-4">Deliverables: {phase.deliverables}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>Remote-first, verified locally</h2>
  <p>
    The paperwork — budgets, contracts, draws, change orders — travels perfectly. It is reviewed centrally by
    the same people every time, which is how consistency is kept across engagements. The site does not
    travel, so site verification is performed by retained inspectors in the project’s region, reporting into
    the same file. The combination is deliberate: continuity of judgment, local eyes on the work.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ready to see where your project sits? <a href="/project-review">Request a project review</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
